dialogue_history.argument:
Automation bias is not an unavoidable consequence of using AI for workplace safety. In safety-critical settings, AI monitoring is typically deployed as decision support within a human-in-the-loop process where:

- humans remain responsible for final judgments (so workers are not encouraged to “trust and stop looking”);
- systems are built with alarm-management and uncertainty/recency cues to reduce false confidence and alert fatigue;
- training and procedures explicitly require independent checks and escalation when AI flags risk;
- organizations audit near-misses and model performance, adjusting thresholds when misses or over-trust patterns appear.

Under these conditions, AI can improve safety by catching anomalies, early signals of failure, and edge-case patterns that humans would not notice—while the human role preserves genuine vigilance. So the claim that AI’s hazard monitoring “does not reliably translate into fewer accidents” depends on the wrong assumption that deployment will necessarily erode human attention; with proper safeguards, AI monitoring can reduce accidents and therefore supports the view that AI is good for society.
